<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the new desk is whole (15-23); the oldest system in the building is next.*

The 2011 helpdesk still writes `legacy.cases`: one wide table, with a lifecycle smeared across `is_closed` and `deleted_at`. It will keep running for eighteen months. It gets the respect an elder system is owed: nobody stops it, nobody mocks it, and every step away from it keeps a way back. You want lesson 8's `Ticket`, a proper resource, **today**, without stopping the old app and with a way back at every step.

The repo's simulated schema (`priv/legacy/`) ships the tables its reference demo needs, and `legacy.cases` is not one of them. Create it the way `priv/legacy/schema.sql` is applied — with `psql`, never as an Ecto migration, because Ash must not own this schema (read `priv/legacy/README.md`; the rule is the whole demonstration). The flags are the ones the repo's own `mix ash_enterprise.legacy.setup` passes: host, port, user and database from the devenv environment (`$PGPORT` is dynamically allocated, so never hardcode 5432), and `--no-password` so a credential problem fails loudly instead of hanging on a prompt:

```bash
devenv shell -- psql --no-password --set ON_ERROR_STOP=1 \
  --host "${PGHOST:-localhost}" --port "$PGPORT" \
  --username "${DB_USER:-postgres}" --dbname "$PGDATABASE" -c "
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS legacy.cases (
  id serial PRIMARY KEY,
  title text NOT NULL,
  is_closed boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false,
  deleted_at timestamp,
  created_at timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  updated_at timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
INSERT INTO legacy.cases (title, is_closed) VALUES
  ('Printer on fire', false),
  ('VPN down for the whole floor', true);"
```

`ash_strangler` maps an Ash resource onto the legacy rows through a writable Postgres view. Declare the mapping once on the resource; derive the view, `INSTEAD OF` triggers (only where needed), expression indexes, a resumable backfill, a drift reconciler and a notification bridge.

The real product is the **compile-time check**: it refuses a mapping where an attribute has no legacy source, a transform has no inverse and does not say so, or a value table is not total.

## Declare the twin and the mapping

First the **twin**: the legacy relation declared as an ordinary Ash resource, so the mapping can name its columns and have them mean something. The repo's own twins live in `AshEnterprise.Legacy.Twins`, generated by `mix ash_strangler.gen.twin` and kept honest by `mix ash_strangler.check`. This one stays in the tutorial directory so cleanup is one `git clean`; register it in the tutorial domain alongside the Refund.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/legacy_cases.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.LegacyCases do
  use Ash.Resource,
    domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
    data_layer: AshPostgres.DataLayer,
    extensions: [AshStrangler.Twin]

  postgres do
    table "cases"
    schema "legacy"
    repo AshEnterprise.Repo
    # Ash does not own this table and never will.
    migrate? false
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]
  end

  attributes do
    attribute :id, :integer, primary_key?: true, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :title, :string, public?: true
    attribute :is_closed, :boolean, public?: true
    attribute :deleted_at, :naive_datetime, public?: true
    attribute :created_at, :naive_datetime, public?: true
    attribute :updated_at, :naive_datetime, public?: true
  end
end
```

Then the read model over it:

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/ticket.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Ticket do
  use AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource,
    domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
    # business_owned, like AshEnterprise.Legacy.User: only that ownership adds
    # the owning_business_unit_id column the mapping's constant supplies.
    ownership: :business_owned,
    lifecycle?: false,
    extra_extensions: [AshStrangler.Resource]

  postgres do
    # The VIEW, in schema `strangler` — not `legacy.cases`. Ash owns the view,
    # never the table underneath.
    table "tutorial_tickets"
    schema "strangler"
    repo AshEnterprise.Repo
    migrate? false
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]
    default_accept []
  end

  attributes do
    # The view computes this value (uuid_v5 of the legacy key), so it is a
    # declared primary key with no default — exactly as AshEnterprise.Legacy.User
    # does it. Without the declaration Ash has no primary key and no read action
    # to read through.
    attribute :id, :uuid, primary_key?: true, allow_nil?: false, writable?: false, public?: true

    attribute :subject, :string, public?: true
    attribute :status, :string, public?: true
  end

  strangler do
    phase :read_from_legacy

    source AshEnterprise.Tutorial.LegacyCases do
      notify? true

      key :id, from: :id, strategy: {:uuid_v5, namespace: "6b1e8b2c-6f6d-4a4a-9f1a-5b0e0d3c4a71"}
      map :subject, from: :title
      map :status,
        from: expr(if(is_closed, do: type("closed", :string), else: type("open", :string))),
        read_only?: true,
        because: "The old app's lifecycle is a boolean; reopening is a decision the new model gets to make, not a column write."

      map :archived_at, from: :deleted_at, zone: "UTC"
      map :created_on, from: :created_at, zone: "UTC"
      map :modified_on, from: :updated_at, zone: "UTC"

      # The legacy estate is one tenant, exactly as in AshEnterprise.Legacy.User.
      constant(:organization_id, expr(type("00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000fe", :uuid)))
      constant(:owning_business_unit_id, expr(type("00000000-0000-0000-0000-0000000000fd", :uuid)))
      constant(:version_number, expr(1))

      unmapped(
        [
          :created_by_id,
          :modified_by_id,
          :created_on_behalf_by_id,
          :modified_on_behalf_by_id,
          :overridden_created_on,
          :import_sequence_number
        ],
        as: :null,
        because: "The 2011 app recorded no actor on any write."
      )
    end
  end
end
```

Register both new resources in the tutorial domain alongside the Refund, then read `AshEnterprise.Legacy.User` — the real strangler this tutorial's repo ships — next to what you just wrote; yours is its outline with the interesting arguments left as an exercise. Saving these files is a DSL change: **restart the app node** (the overview says why `recompile()` will not do).

The mapping never reaches `mix ash.codegen`: `migrate? false` is what keeps it out, and a repo test refuses any codegen output that mentions `legacy` at all (`priv/legacy/README.md`). The view DDL comes from the strangler generator. In a terminal:

```bash
devenv shell -- mix ash_strangler.gen.migration --name add_cases_compatibility_view
```

<div class="rounded-xl p-4" style="background-color: #fff4e5; color: #61758a;">

> **Reduce the generated migration to your resource.** The generator re-emits the compatibility views for *every* twin in the repo, not just yours — and the repo's own `strangler.parties` view has camelCase columns (`createdAt`) that Postgres folds to lowercase in the emitted DDL, so the migration can fail on a view you never touched (`column "createdat" of relation "parties" does not exist`, ERROR 42703). The documented step is: delete every generated block except your resource's — for this lesson, the `tutorial_tickets` view, its expression index, and the notify trigger — then migrate. Do not hand-fix the generator's SQL for resources you do not own; that is what `mix ash_strangler.check` and the repo's CI are for.

</div>

```bash
devenv shell -- mix ecto.migrate
```

What survives your reduction is a view in schema `strangler` over `legacy.cases`, an expression index for the uuid key, and — because `notify? true` — the `AFTER` trigger that fires `pg_notify`. The migration ran against a table that existed before it, which is the sequencing `mix.exs`' aliases exist to guarantee for `legacy.users` too. Now read through the view, as the legacy estate's own admin. Provision that admin the way a deploy does — the platform's estate seeder is idempotent and, unlike the mix task, works from the attached node because the running server has the endpoint the confirmation email needs:

```elixir
require Ash.Query

estate_tenant = AshEnterprise.Legacy.Estate.organization_id()

legacy_admin =
  case AshEnterprise.Platform.Seeder.seed_legacy_estate() do
    %{user: user} -> user
    :already_seeded -> nil
  end ||
    (AshEnterprise.Accounts.User
     |> Ash.Query.filter(email == "admin@legacy.example")
     |> Ash.read_one!(authorize?: false))

AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Ticket
|> Ash.read!(actor: legacy_admin, tenant: estate_tenant)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.subject, &1.status})
```

If `legacy_admin` comes back `nil`, a previous `mix ash_enterprise.seed` died mid-run and left the estate half-provisioned (its organization exists, its admin does not); rebuild the dev database with `mix ecto.reset` and re-run lesson 15's actor/tenant cell — it is idempotent.

Even the table you do not own is modelled the Ash way: policies, tenancy and your agent's `describe` all apply.

## Exercise: make it refuse

Add an attribute `:priority` with no mapping and compile. Then add a `concat` that is not decomposable without `read_only?: true` and a `because:`.

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  Both fail to compile, with the attribute named. The fix for the first is a mapping or an explicit statement that it is new-only; for the second:

  ```elixir
  concat :display, from: [:title, :ref], separator: " - ",
    read_only?: true,
    because: "Titles contain ' - '; no separator splits it back."
  ```

  `because:` is for the reviewer. The compiler wants the decision written down.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** Two compile errors, each naming the missing mapping or the missing `because:` — the refusal is the product.

## Live from day one

Update a row from the legacy side with raw SQL, as the old app would:

```elixir
AshEnterprise.Repo.query!("UPDATE legacy.cases SET is_closed = true WHERE id = 1")
```

`pg_notify` carries the key; Ash **re-reads through the view**, so calculations, policies and tenancy apply, and emits a real `Ash.Notifier.Notification`. Your A2UI surface (23) or LiveView (12) updates. A write from 2011 code lands in a UI you wrote last week.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> The 2011 app is my old neighbor. Tonight one of its writes walked into the new world wearing a uuid, and nobody stopped the old app to do it. The phases have a retirement in them; no funeral required.</p>
</div>

## Phases are the one knob

| Phase | Reads from | Writes to | Safe to change |
|---|---|---|---|
| `:read_from_legacy` | legacy, via the view | nothing new | freely |
| `:dual_write` | legacy | both | after backfill starts |
| `:read_from_new` | new tables | both | **only when backfill is complete** |
| `:decommissioned` | new tables | new tables | only when nothing writes legacy |

Moving to `:read_from_new` with an incomplete backfill gives missing rows, not an error. An agent that does not know a resource's phase cannot judge whether a change is safe, so ask: `AshAgentTools.describe_resource(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Ticket)`.

In `ash_enterprise` the legacy estate is **projected, not cut over** (ADR 0031): the old system is a source the new model reads, until it is not.

When you are finished with the whole tutorial, the overview's **When you're done** recipe drops the view and trigger with the migration and removes the twin and the Ticket with `lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/`. The `legacy.cases` table itself stays: it belongs to the schema Ash does not own, and that is the point.

